        exp: "Rule: At traditional common law, arson is defined as the malicious burning of the dwelling house of another[cite: 28]. The common law strictly restricted the crime to structures used for human habitation (or buildings within the residential curtilage)[cite: 28]. A commercial standalone auto repair garage is an industrial business property, NOT a dwelling house[cite: 28]. While modern statutory codes in virtually all jurisdictions have expanded arson to include commercial structures, motor vehicles, and personal property, under the explicit common law definition tested here, burning an unoccupied commercial building cannot constitute common law arson (Option C)[cite: 28]. Option A is incorrect because charring structural wood satisfies the burning element, but fails the indispensable dwelling house element[cite: 28]. Option B is incorrect because transferred intent does not expand the substantive statutory scope of common law arson beyond dwellings[cite: 28]. Option D is incorrect because charring interior structural framing satisfies the burning requirement; liability fails strictly because the building was not a dwelling[cite: 28]."
